request: In the watchlist, show movies I haven't watched yet before the ones I've watched.

governor turn 1: plan -> watchlist  (This is a display-order concern for the watchlist page: deriving a sorted view in the watchlist component keeps the store contract (and the persisted list order) untouched, while the rendered list shows unwatched movies first. No exported signature changes.)
round 1: owners watchlist=ok; oracle RED
governor turn 2: plan -> watchlist  (The unwatched-first ordering landed (tsc clean, acceptance tests green), but the watchlist change regressed the two existing DOM click tests, which specify toggle behaviour, not order — so they must pass unchanged. Re-instruct watchlist to fix the interaction regression while keeping the ordering.)
round 2: owners watchlist=rejected; oracle RED
governor turn 3: plan -> watchlist  (The two failing tests hard-code the pre-feature assumption that the clicked row stays at index 0; the requested unwatched-before-watched ordering necessarily moves a freshly watched row into the watched group, so their post-click index-0 re-query reads a different movie's unchecked checkbox and cannot pass against any correct implementation. The owner's proposed modifications preserve the tests' intent (a click on the checkbox or on blank row space toggles that movie exactly once, still via the real DOM) and add assertions that the same DOM row stays bound to the same movie while it moves to the watched group, so both are approved. Production code already conforms to the accepted ordering contract (computed unwatched-first sort of a copy of store items, track by movie.id, click guards intact) and stays as-is; my acceptance test is extended to pin the click-then-reorder behaviour independently of the owner's spec file.)
round 3: owners watchlist=ok; oracle GREEN
governor turn 4: accept  (Oracle is green (tsc clean, 32 tests pass), including the ordering acceptance tests and the two approved test modifications needed for runtime reordering. The watchlist now lists unwatched movies before watched ones.)

